    "turn_idx": 46,
    "turn_text": "I actually don't have a bigger problem as some people do with this. I really don't. The power of the pardon is absolute. And if you want to do it preemptively, what you're doing basically saying is we're pardoning you from any and all crimes you may or may not have committed. And if this is what Joe wants to do out the door and he wants this to be his legacy, I'm fine with it. He can pardon himself too. Because frankly, there is real reason, warranted reason for him. He lied through his teeth about Parisma. He said, I never talked to his son, his brother or anybody for that matter. His own son implicates him. His son would not have the right to plead the fifth, by the way, considering he's already been pardoned. And if, honestly, if this is what they want to do, Um, let them do it. The power I just respect the power of the pardon. And frankly, I'd like to see Donald Trump use the power of the presidential pardon in new ways that, you know, he started a little bit in his first term. I'd like to see him expand on him. and get somebody like Alice Marie Johnson maker, the pardons are and Alice Marie Johnson and find people that have changed in jail that have served a significant portion of their term and deserve a second chance. You can also find people that were wrongly convicted or people that were victims of a weaponized justice system and and give them their freedom back. I can't take anything worse than losing your freedom. So I don't really have a problem with it.",
